## English Version

**AI & Research · 28 October 2026 · 14 min read · Nguyen Ngoc Thien**

# Why Prompt Guardrails Fail Longitudinal Health AI—and Why Safety Belongs at the Commit Boundary

*Evaluating a health-AI assistant on single-turn Q&A is like testing a database with one `SELECT` and never testing concurrent writes. A candid technical breakdown of Snapshot-Bound Commit Admission (GLHS)—accepted as Short Paper #97 at HEALTHINF 2027—and why write-time governance beats prompt policing.*

---

#### 01. The single-turn illusion everyone keeps benchmarking

Whenever I talk about my work on longitudinal health AI, the first question I get is usually some version of: *"Wait—aren't frontier LLMs already scoring above 90% on medical licensing exams? Isn't the accuracy problem mostly solved?"*

My answer is always the same: licensing exams test whether a model can recite medical knowledge when every single relevant fact is frozen, verified, and handed to it inside one neat prompt. Real longitudinal care looks nothing like a multiple-choice exam.

In real healthcare, truth moves asynchronously across weeks and months. A clinical fact may become valid in the patient's body at one moment—what medical database researchers call *valid time* ($t_v$)—and only become known to the system days later at *knowledge time* ($t_k$). Two clinical sources can genuinely disagree while an investigation is still open. And a piece of sensitive information that is appropriate for one actor, role, and clinical task can be completely unauthorized for another.

Even worse, the danger does not end when retrieval succeeds. Suppose your pipeline retrieves the exact right records at 9:00 AM and shows an authorized view to an AI assistant. The assistant reasons over that view and returns a proposed medication update or care-plan edit minutes or hours later. During that quiet gap between read and write, the patient's medication list may have been updated by another clinician, a delayed lab panel may have arrived, the patient may have withdrawn consent, or the governing policy epoch may have moved on.

If your system blindly merges the model's proposal, you don't have a "medical hallucination." You have a stale, unauthorized state transition dressed in fluent clinical prose.

---

#### 02. The seam between retrieval and persistence

One thing I always want to be brutally clear about—especially because the HEALTHINF 2027 reviewers rightly pushed on novelty and positioning—is what **GLHS** does *and does not* claim to invent.

Health informatics already has strong, battle-tested building blocks. HL7 FHIR models Provenance, Consent, and version-aware HTTP operations; openEHR preserves versioned clinical contributions; W3C PROV provides a general provenance framework; and recent agent-memory systems explore bitemporal arbitration and transactional belief updates.

GLHS does not rebrand those mechanisms as new inventions. Instead, it points a spotlight at a narrow, dangerous seam between two operations that are usually engineered in isolation:

* **A version check alone is blind to disclosure.** Matching a base state version can reject a stale write, yet it cannot prove *which governed disclosure* supplied the evidence and policy context behind the model's proposal.


* **A disclosure policy alone vanishes after inference.** A privacy-aware retriever can minimize what the model sees at read time, yet disappear the moment the model hands back an update to persist.



Without a contract joining those two ends, the read-to-write interval becomes a governance blind spot. Proposals can land against stale state versions, outlive revoked consent or expired authorizations, or rely on evidence that was never legitimately disclosed for that task.

---

#### 03. Formalizing the read-to-write contract: THSS $\to$ GST

The core architectural choice in **GLHS** (formalized in *Snapshot-Bound Commit Admission for Longitudinal Health AI*, Short Paper #97 at HEALTHINF 2027) is refusal: **never ask the language model to grade its own safety, and never grant it raw database write authority**. Every governance check runs on a deterministic, non-LLM path.

On the **read path**, a policy-aware gateway resolves the governed longitudinal state at explicit clinical valid-time ($t_v$) and knowledge-time ($t_k$) cutoffs—keeping database write time strictly as an audit coordinate rather than a proxy for knowledge time. It compiles and persists a **Task-Bounded Health State Snapshot (THSS)** before the model ever runs:

$$H = (u, \alpha, r, \phi, \tau, v_s, v_\pi, v_c, t_v, t_k, id_H, d_H, E_H)$$

Here, $u$ is the subject profile; $\alpha$ and $r$ are actor and role; $\phi$ and $\tau$ are purpose and task; $v_s, v_\pi, v_c$ pin the state, policy, and consent versions; $id_H$ is the persisted snapshot identity; $E_H$ is the disclosed evidence set; and $d_H$ is a SHA-256 consistency digest computed over a strict project-defined canonical JSON profile (sorted keys, preserved array order, compact UTF-8, UTC-normalized datetimes, normalized negative zero, failing closed on any mismatch—explicitly documented as project-defined rather than RFC 8785).

On the **write path**, the model outputs free-form prose only as explanation, never as an executable mutation. To change state, it emits a structured proposal $P$ referencing $H$, its declared dependency set, its delta $\Delta$, and provenance $\rho$. A **Governed State Transition (GST)** gateway then enforces the commit-admission invariant:

$$\text{Commit}(P) \iff \text{Bound}(P, H) \land \text{StateCurrent}(P) \land \text{GovernanceCurrent}(P) \land \text{SnapshotValid}(P)$$

Rather than collapsing every rejection into a vague database error, the gateway returns a typed failure vocabulary: a mismatch in $id_H$ or $d_H$ signals tampering or snapshot substitution; a moved $v_s$ signals stale state; a changed $v_c$ or $v_\pi$ signals governance drift; an expired window rejects a proposal that outlived its authorization; and a dependency-set mismatch catches evidence outside the disclosed scope.

---

#### 04. Separating three evidence classes—and refusing to oversell the results

If there is one habit I am proudest of in this paper, it is refusing to lump software conformance, database concurrency, and LLM reasoning into one flashy headline metric. In our evaluation, we deliberately kept three evidence classes strictly apart:

1. **Deterministic Contract Conformance (112 cells):** We ran 16 developer-authored cases across 7 progressively stronger contract variants. Every single cell produced the expected outcome and pinned the exact first-rejecting clause—catching mismatches in profile, actor, role, purpose, task, base version, snapshot identity, and digest. In a historical replay check, an earlier conflict remained faithfully visible even after a later resolution, because visibility was reconstructed from transition history rather than a flattened mutable projection.


2. **PostgreSQL 16.14 Concurrency & Service Cost (50 races, 310 writer attempts):** Using a profile-global state version counter across $W \in \{1, 2, 4, 8, 16\}$ concurrent writers (five races per level), every tested stale-write race closed cleanly with one atomic winner and zero database errors. Just as importantly, the experiment exposed the real engineering price of simplicity: for writes touching *unrelated* slots on the same profile, false-stale rejection climbed along the exact one-winner curve—$0$ at 1 writer, $0.50$ at 2, $0.75$ at 4, $0.875$ at 8, and $0.9375$ at 16. On a single local in-process worker (excluding HTTP and LLM inference, 30 sequential runs), p50/p95 latency was 61.81/68.89 ms for THSS compilation, 30.97/54.69 ms for GST transitions, 7.30/12.30 ms for state reconstruction, and 2.12/5.50 ms for audit lookups, with peak process RSS around 109 MiB.


3. **Prospective Two-Model Synthetic Cohort (64 cases, 1,152 cells):** To test whether strict task-bounded disclosure hurts reasoning utility, we evaluated Claude and Gemini across Strict THSS and five context controls—full authorized history, long chronological context, Naive RAG, last-write-wins (LWW), and BTSA—scored on a strict all-four-axis exactness endpoint (lifecycle, evidence, timeliness, escalation). All 1,152 cells completed without execution errors. Under Strict THSS, **Gemini was exact on 64/64 cases (100%)** and **Claude on 63/64 cases (98.44%)**—and we deliberately left that single Claude error (a cancelled history predicted as satisfied) untouched without post-hoc repair. Across paired Holm-adjusted comparisons ($\alpha = 0.05$), Strict THSS beat Naive RAG (21/0/43 wins/losses/ties on Claude; 16/0/48 on Gemini) and LWW (16/0/48 on both), though Claude did not significantly outperform BTSA and Gemini tied BTSA, full history, and long context.



---

#### 05. Listening to the reviewers—and why honesty is the real engineering standard

Getting accepted to HEALTHINF 2027 with reviewer scores spanning 4 to 6 across relevance, originality, meaningfulness, and presentation was validating. What actually made the work sharper, though, were the hard questions the reviewers put in front of me.

They pointed out—accurately—what this paper *does not* yet prove:

* 64 synthetic cases with high tie rates left only 9–21 informative discordant pairs in significant contrasts, underpowered relative to our planned target of 51.


* Atomicity against *concurrent governance writers* (authorization loss, consent revocation, or policy updates racing mid-commit) was not yet stress-tested.


* A base-version-only proposal path remains exposed in the gateway, meaning provenance-sensitive downgrade prevention is not yet enforced across every downstream route.


* Most fundamentally, software conformance on synthetic cases is **not** clinical validation or legal compliance.



Anyone claiming an early-stage health-AI prototype is "clinically proven" is selling marketing copy, not science. People still ask me: *"Won't next year's frontier model simply be smart enough not to make stale or unauthorized writes?"* My answer hasn't changed: no. Concurrency, stale reads, and revoked consent are not intelligence deficits. A senior physician with thirty years of practice can still overwrite a chart if the EHR hides a lab report that landed two minutes earlier.

Better models sharpen reasoning inside the read window. Deterministic contracts protect durable state at the write boundary. Keeping those two jobs separate is what turns an impressive prototype into software you can actually audit.

> *"Safety in longitudinal AI is not decided when a model generates a fluent clinical paragraph—it is decided at the exact millisecond an inference asks to become durable state."*

